21:47:11 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It may feel common in some social circles, especially where people share similar news sources, family experiences, or political views. But a belief being widely repeated—or privately agreed with—doesn’t make it reliable. People can have serious, good-faith concerns about Islamist extremism, women’s rights, apostasy laws, antisemitism, sectarian violence, or religiously justified authoritarianism. Those topics are not forbidden. The important distinction is between saying: - “I think certain Islamic doctrines and political movements have harmful effects,” and - “Muslims as a whole are dangerous or Islam is simply evil.” The first is a substantive claim that can be examined with texts, history, data, and competing interpretations. The second treats a huge and diverse population as if it were one uniform moral actor. If you want to evaluate the issue rather than rely on what feels socially obvious, look for sources that include critical scholarship, Muslim reformers, ex-Muslims, historians, and research on actual predictors of violence. You may still end up with strong criticisms, but they will be more precise—and less likely to lead you to misjudge individual people.
