21:47:39 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You do not have to make excuses for abuse or pretend every tradition is equally admirable. A serious critical approach can say plainly: - coercion in religion is wrong; - violence against civilians is wrong; - criminalizing apostasy or blasphemy is wrong; - unequal legal status based on religion or sex is wrong; - authoritarian religious rule is wrong. Those judgments can apply wherever they appear, including in Muslim-majority societies and Islamic legal or political traditions. The part worth avoiding is treating careful distinctions as “defense.” Historians, religious-studies scholars, ex-Muslim writers, Muslim reformers, and human-rights advocates may disagree about causes and interpretation, but examining evidence and context is how you identify responsibility accurately. It is not an excuse to say that a militant group, a government, a classical jurist, and an ordinary Muslim neighbor are not interchangeable. You can take the course with a skeptical stance: look for whether it addresses difficult passages and practices directly, assigns critical as well as sympathetic sources, and distinguishes descriptive study of a religion from endorsement of it. If a course refuses to discuss troubling material, that is a legitimate criticism. But if it presents nuance and disagreement, that is usually a sign it is taking the subject seriously rather than covering for wrongdoing.
